ints that make this a challenging property to redevelop most notably and you can see this most clearly on exhibit one the property does not have any direct direct street access so it actually gains access to dillerville road via an easement over the adjoining shopping center to the south of the property so as a result of that we've been sensitive to the need to find a use for this property that would have relatively low trip generation and that is how we landed on a self-storage facility as the proposed use self-storage facilities are not permitted in the c3 district they are permitted in the adjoining cm district and so we are asking as part of our request to extend the cm zoning district to encompass this property in addition the facility that we're proposing to build is a little bit different than what i'll call a traditional drive up self-storage facility it's more in keeping with current trends to build taller facilities with more interior access to units and the zoning ordinance the city zoning ordinance doesn't really contemplate that kind of facility so currently the ordinance would limit all facilities to 20 000 square feet or less and the facility we're proposing to develop would be three stories and about 120 000 square feet so we're proposing to in addition to the rezoning to modify the regulations in the cm district to allow for a facility of this type in certain circumstances within the cm district and we did work extensively with staff on developing the criteria that would need to be met in order for this type of facility to be developed in the cm district specifically so i'll just run through those quickly now so first of all the property on which the facility is constructed would need to be between three and seven acres you may have heard me mention that this condo unit or planned community unit is only two and a half acres but the overall property um that the the tax parcel that the property uh includes is is within those measurements between three